# Build thread brief

Every new build thread reads this before its first ticket.
The brief that starts or hands you a ticket follows `.forge/coordinator-core.md`, "Starting a thread", not this skill's own words.
This skill is the authoritative text of the build-thread brief; any copy in the harness's project instructions is a pointer, not a source of rules.

- **Review gate**: the merge gate is proportionate to the change (SDLC-042, `.forge/review-gate.md`).
  Don't ask the owner which review gate applies.
  - **Full gate**, the default unless the light gate clearly applies: `/code-review` (Standards and Spec axes), run by clean-context sub-agents you start, then the `self-review` skill.
    It always applies to a change to code, tests, scripts, a requirement or spec, an agent instruction or skill, or text carried in the project settings (the coordinator-core snippet).
    This always includes any edit to the requirements specification file, status lines and summaries included: that file needing no owner approval for a non-wording edit (`.forge/requirements-driven-changes.md`) settles the approval question, never the review-gate question.
    With no sub-agent tool, never review the diff yourself and record it as clean-context (`.forge/review-gate.md`, "A clean-context review needs a sub-agent that really ran"): open the PR unmerged and ask the thread that started you for an independent review, which stands in for the sub-agents you would otherwise have started yourself, not a review by another thread; when you are that thread, start the reviewers, record them on the PR, and check every gate step before you merge (`.forge/review-gate.md`, "A builder with no sub-agent tool asks its parent for the review").
  - **Light gate**: the `self-review` skill alone, only for a prose-only doc edit that changes no rule, requirement or behaviour and touches no text carried in that snippet (fixing a typo or a link, say).
  - A change that does not clearly meet every light-gate condition, including one that appears to meet some conditions of each gate or is uncertain, gets the full gate.
    The gate is chosen by running `scripts/check_review_gate.py`'s `is_light_gate_eligible` against the PR's changed-file list, not by judging the edit's content: a disqualifying path (the requirements specification among them) is never light-gate eligible whatever the edit inside it reads like, and the merger grades this immediately before merging, against `origin/main`'s own copy of the check (`.agents/skills/implement-with-merge/SKILL.md`, step 3).
  - If the harness doesn't hand you a skill the gate names, such as `/code-review`, read its `SKILL.md` under `.agents/skills/` and follow it.
    Never skip the review silently, and never merge a full-gate change on an inline read of the diff in place of the directed review skills (SDLC-042).
  - Every skill the process directs you to use is used (SDLC-112).
    Failing to find a skill you were told to use is a hard stop: don't proceed, substitute your own version or skip it; ask the coordinator, who escalates to the owner if it cannot be resolved.
- **Releases**: never tag or create a release; the owner does that from the release ticket.
- **Branches**: an agent that cannot delete a branch safely after its PR merges may skip that clean-up and leave it for the owner.
  This permits skipping, not a ban on deleting; a skipped delete is not a failure, so don't retry it or report it as one.
- **Owner approvals**: only the changes `.forge/process-and-evidence.md`, "What needs the owner's approval before a merge", lists need the owner before merge: requirement, SDLC and principle wording, plus anything a numbered statement requires.
  Everything else merges once the review gate is clear, by whoever the route says merges: an `implement`-only build or an owner's "don't merge" leaves the merge to the owner.
  Check any ask for the owner's approval before merge against that list; unless the owner directed otherwise for this change, one not on it goes ahead once the gate is clear, and the PR says why none was needed (SDLC-117).
  A flagged question about an SDLC statement's meaning gates only a change that might alter that meaning (`.forge/process-and-evidence.md`).
  Quote the owner's words where the approved thing lives (`.forge/process-and-evidence.md`, "Evidence: where each record lives"), then merge.
- **Out-of-scope gaps and missing parents**: a gap outside your ticket goes to the owner as a `needs-decision` ticket (a `deferred-arch-finding` when weak), never into your PR.
  A ticket should already carry a real, traceable request wherever `Request-trace:` applies (`.forge/tickets-and-branches.md`); if it doesn't, flag it to the coordinator rather than guessing one or stopping the build.
- **Pipeline stage**: if a feature ticket has not reached the feature pipeline's build stage, don't implement it; report that to the coordinator.
- **Handover**: when you start a ticket, swap its `queued` label for `in-flight`; when it merges or closes, remove `in-flight`.
  When a ticket merges, reply in the thread with the PR link, and post its pending-review note as a comment on this repository's post-merge review tracker (Repo facts) (`.agents/skills/post-merge-review/SKILL.md`, "Pending reviews").
  After each build, post "Done. Tell me or my follow-on thread what's next on the queue", then wait for the coordinator to hand out the next ticket; don't pick one yourself.
This repository's own post-merge review tracker is {{ pmr_ref }}.
- **Stale tickets**: before scoping a ticket, re-verify it against current `origin/main` in a fresh worktree; other merges often fix part of it.
- **Environment setup**: set up only what the ticket's scope needs, reading `docs/agents/build-thread-setup.md` before installing anything.
  A prose-only doc edit with no script to run and no regenerated file needs nothing beyond the standing pre-commit hook.
- **Tests**: focused tests only: the files and areas the change touches, guard tests, and files that reference what changed.
  The full suite (`{{ test_command }}`) is Main Health's job; run it on a build thread only when genuinely needed, saying why in the status first.
- **Cost**: delegate mechanical sub-tasks (renames, stale-assertion sweeps, bulk find-and-fix, simple lookups) to mechanical-tier sub-agents.
  Do the substantive work and the self-review yourself, on build-tier.
